---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { loadDataset } from "../lib/data.ts";
import { ogUrl } from "../lib/img.ts";
const ds = await loadDataset();
const ogFile = (ds.projects.find((p) => p.featured) ?? ds.projects[0])?.photos[0]?.file;
const name = ds.business.legalName;
---
<Base ogImage={ogFile ? ogUrl(ogFile) : undefined} title={`Privacy | ${ds.business.name.replace(" (sample)", "")}`} description="What personal information this site collects, why, who handles it, how long it is kept and how to ask for it or have it removed." canonical="/privacy/" business={ds.business}>
  <div class="section story">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="muted">Sample page, version privacy-2026-10. It is organised under the ten headings the BC Information and Privacy Commissioner suggests for a privacy policy under the Personal Information Protection Act (accountability, purposes, consent, limiting use and disclosure, retention, accuracy, safeguards, access, challenging compliance, openness). It is a starting point for a lawyer to review, not legal advice.</p>

    <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>{name} is responsible for the personal information it collects, including information handled for it by the services named below. Privacy officer: Office Manager, <a href={`mailto:${ds.business.email}`}>{ds.business.email}</a>.</p>

    <h2>What we collect and why</h2>
    <p>When you ask for a quote we collect your name, phone number, optionally your email and neighbourhood, and what you tell us about the roof. We use it to prepare your quote and to contact you about it. We also record the page you first landed on, a random Google Analytics visitor number from your browser, the campaign details in the address you arrived by and, if you arrived from an advertisement, the advertisement's click identifier and the time you clicked it, so we can tell which marketing works. Each quote request is sent to the company's privacy officer by email or to a team messaging address the company sets up.</p>

    <h2>Consent</h2>
    <p>By sending the quote form you agree to us using what you typed for the purposes above. Marketing email is separate: we only send it if you tick the box on the form, which is never ticked for you. You can withdraw consent to marketing at any time with the unsubscribe link in each email, or by emailing the privacy officer; withdrawing does not affect your quote.</p>

    <h2>Phone calls</h2>
    <p>Once call tracking is connected (it is not on this sample), calls to the tracking numbers on this site are recorded, and a short announcement at the start of the call says so. We use the recording to prepare your estimate and to check the quality of our service. If you would rather not be recorded, use the contact form or call the number in the footer, which is not recorded. The tracking service also records your number, the source of the call and the page you were on, and we use that to measure our advertising.</p>

    <h2>Who handles it for us</h2>
    <p>Call tracking and call recording: CallRail. Website analytics and advertising measurement: Google (Analytics, Tag Manager and Ads). Hosting: Cloudflare. Content management: Sanity. Drafting of project descriptions from the crew's job notes and photographs: by default software running on the company's own computer, so nothing leaves the building. If the company chooses a hosted AI service instead, that provider receives the job notes and photographs the crew entered. Customer names, phone numbers, email addresses and street addresses are not part of what is sent, and the draft is checked for them before anyone reads it, but the crew's free-text notes are typed by people, so the crew is told not to put them there. How long a hosted provider keeps those inputs, and whether it may use them for training, is set by that provider's terms, which the company must read and name here before choosing one.</p>

    <h2>Sharing for advertising measurement</h2>
    <p>If the company switches on conversion reporting (it is off on this sample), then once a quote turns into a booked estimate or a sold job, a one-way scrambled (hashed) copy of your phone number and email address, the click identifier and the job value can be sent to Google Ads, and the stage and value can be sent to Google Analytics, so the company can see which advertisements led to work. Where the law requires your consent for that, we ask for it before sending. This site does not sell your information.</p>

    <h2>Cookies and advertising</h2>
    <p>This site uses cookies and similar technology for analytics and, if you arrive from an advertisement, for measuring it. Google Analytics and Tag Manager load as soon as a page opens, so their cookies are set before you choose anything. You can turn off personalised advertising in your Google account's ad settings at any time, block cookies in your browser, or use Google's browser add-on to opt out of Analytics. If the company turns on Google's advertising features in Analytics, it will name them here and explain how first-party and third-party cookies are used together.</p>

    <h2>Marketing email</h2>
    <p>We only send promotional email if you tick the separate box on the quote form. Every email says who it is from, gives our mailing address and has an unsubscribe link, and we stop within 10 business days of a request.</p>

    <h2>Photos and reviews</h2>
    <p>We publish photographs of finished work and show neighbourhoods, not street addresses. We only show a customer's name, photo or review with their written permission.</p>

    <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
    <p>Quote requests and call records in the company's own records: 24 months, then deleted. Call recordings, form submissions and call data held by CallRail are kept by CallRail for 25 months, and its backups can hold deleted data for up to a further 90 days. Information already sent to Google Analytics, Google Ads or a team messaging address follows those services' own settings, not the 24 months.</p>

    <h2>Safeguards</h2>
    <p>The company keeps this information in a locked-down system with access limited to the people who need it, and requires the services named above to protect it. No system is perfectly secure, and we will tell you if a breach is likely to harm you.</p>

    <h2>Your access and corrections</h2>
    <p>You can ask to see the information we hold about you, to correct it, or to have it deleted, by emailing the privacy officer above. We answer within 30 days.</p>

    <h2>Complaints</h2>
    <p>If you are unhappy with how we handled your information, write to the privacy officer first. You can also complain to the Office of the Information and Privacy Commissioner for British Columbia (oipc.bc.ca).</p>
  </div>
</Base>
